# faulter — a program whose whole job is to take a page fault.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The roadmap has carried "`bg-fault` on-iron survival" as an
# Uncertain item since 1.50.9 — the question being whether a faulted background
# (`&`) job gets torn down cleanly or halts the shell along with it. It rode
# "the next burn" and no CHANGELOG entry since records an outcome, and the
# reason is simple: NOTHING IN THE ROOTFS FAULTS ON PURPOSE. The item had no
# stimulus, so every burn it "rode" could only have reported the absence of a
# fault it never caused. ⇒ A gate whose stimulus cannot fire reports nothing,
# forever, while looking like a check that passed.
#
# WHAT IT DOES. Announces itself, then dereferences a null pointer. The announce
# comes FIRST and is unbuffered so the log distinguishes the two failure shapes
# that would otherwise look identical:
#   - no FAULTER-ALIVE line  -> the program never ran (spawn/exec problem)
#   - FAULTER-ALIVE, no more -> it ran and faulted, which is the intended stimulus
# Without that line, "the shell printed nothing" is unattributable.
#
# ⚠ IT MUST NEVER EXIT NORMALLY. An exit code would make this a well-behaved
# program that merely returns non-zero, and the shell's teardown path for a
# faulted job would go untested while the burn read as green. The store below is
# the last thing that happens; the trailing exit is unreachable and is there only
# so the entry shape matches every other agnos test program.

fn main(): i64 {
    sys_write(1, "FAULTER-ALIVE\n", 14);

    # Address 0 is never mapped in any agnos address space — the low arena starts
    # at PD[128]. A write (not a read) so the fault is unambiguous even if some
    # future zero-page mapping is ever introduced for reads.
    store64(0, 1);

    # Unreachable. If this line is ever reached the test is VACUOUS and the burn
    # result means nothing — the log will say so rather than exiting 0 quietly.
    sys_write(1, "FAULTER-DID-NOT-FAULT\n", 22);
    return 42;
}

fn _entry(): i64 { var r = main(); syscall(SYS_EXIT, r); return 0; }
_entry();
